1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,600
What if an AI gives a convincing answer, but starts from the wrong assumptions?

2
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:17,195
That question is at the heart of PIM, the Prajna Insight Model, and Prajna AI: a
research project exploring how reasoning can become more testable,

3
00:00:17,195 --> 00:00:19,680
transparent, and accountable.

4
00:00:19,680 --> 00:00:29,367
Our Development Roadmap, version zero point two, focuses on three connected ideas:
testable hypotheses, active experimentation,

5
00:00:29,367 --> 00:00:32,515
and an evidence-based reasoning loop.

6
00:00:32,515 --> 00:00:35,445
The goal goes beyond producing an answer.

7
00:00:35,445 --> 00:00:41,649
We want a system that can explain which assumptions it relies on, identify evidence
that could challenge them,

8
00:00:41,649 --> 00:00:46,110
and recognize when the available information is insufficient.

9
00:00:46,110 --> 00:00:49,798
Consider a simple example: the ground is wet.

10
00:00:49,798 --> 00:00:53,223
Was it rain, a sprinkler, or something else?

11
00:00:53,223 --> 00:00:56,954
Generating plausible explanations is only the beginning.

12
00:00:56,954 --> 00:01:01,614
The next challenge is choosing an experiment that can distinguish between them.

13
00:01:01,614 --> 00:01:10,429
PIM's roadmap proposes starting with small, explicitly defined synthetic environments
where the rules and outcomes can be checked.

14
00:01:10,429 --> 00:01:12,800
Propose competing hypotheses.

15
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:16,248
Record predictions before observing results.

16
00:01:16,248 --> 00:01:18,500
Choose informative experiments.

17
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:21,318
Check outcomes and counterexamples.

18
00:01:21,318 --> 00:01:25,200
Then revise hypotheses while preserving the evidence.

19
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:28,500
A failed prediction should remain part of the record.

20
00:01:28,500 --> 00:01:35,156
Changing an explanation should never quietly change the original task or its success
criteria.

21
00:01:35,156 --> 00:01:37,085
What have we built so far?

22
00:01:37,085 --> 00:01:45,584
Our research prototypes include finite symbolic search, explicit contract and
constraint checks, operation-cost accounting,

23
00:01:45,584 --> 00:01:53,095
auditable execution records, and an offline end-to-end prototype comparing three
scripted policies.

24
00:01:53,095 --> 00:02:01,457
Our latest scoring-alignment component also completed a bounded engineering acceptance
check: seventy-three tests passed.

25
00:02:01,457 --> 00:02:12,564
It separates answer completeness, ambiguity and clarification, empty results,
unanswered or unknown states, and matches to curator-selected references.

26
00:02:12,564 --> 00:02:16,125
Those tests verify specified software behavior.

27
00:02:16,125 --> 00:02:21,678
They do not establish general understanding or scientific discovery capabilities.

28
00:02:21,678 --> 00:02:23,189
What comes next?

29
00:02:23,189 --> 00:02:29,402
We plan to investigate whether active experiment selection improves on fixed or random
strategies,

30
00:02:29,402 --> 00:02:33,650
whether additional reasoning costs deliver measurable benefits,

31
00:02:33,650 --> 00:02:39,268
and whether hypotheses can be revised consistently when contradictory evidence
appears.

32
00:02:39,268 --> 00:02:43,676
These are research questions, not achievements we are claiming today.

33
00:02:43,676 --> 00:02:51,825
We also retain negative findings: the historical finite-domain pilot did not
demonstrate a PIM performance advantage.

34
00:02:51,825 --> 00:02:59,008
PIM is currently a system-level research prototype, not a newly trained foundation
language model.

35
00:02:59,008 --> 00:03:08,777
Its potential applications include requirements clarification, diagnosis in finite
rule-based environments, and more inspectable research workflows.

36
00:03:08,777 --> 00:03:12,425
Their practical value still needs controlled evaluation.

37
00:03:12,425 --> 00:03:15,884
The standard we want every claim to meet is straightforward.

38
00:03:15,884 --> 00:03:17,215
How would you test it?

39
00:03:17,215 --> 00:03:19,568
And what evidence would change your mind?

40
00:03:19,568 --> 00:03:26,245
If an AI could run only three experiments, how would you judge whether it had
genuinely reduced uncertainty?

41
00:03:26,245 --> 00:03:32,543
We welcome thoughtful discussion from researchers, engineers, and people working
across disciplines.

42
00:03:32,543 --> 00:03:37,458
Explore the version zero point two roadmap through the link in the accompanying
description.

43
00:03:37,458 --> 00:03:42,639
This introduction and the accompanying concept poster were created with AI assistance.

44
00:03:42,639 --> 00:03:45,456
The poster illustrates the research roadmap.

45
00:03:45,456 --> 00:03:49,591
It does not imply that every capability is already implemented.

46
00:03:49,591 --> 00:03:52,751
This narration uses an AI-generated voice.
